WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.020
You were taught home furnishings, different periods of furniture, how you could judge when a piece of furniture was a good piece or not.

2
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:17.840
We were taught how to read blueprints and we even had to make a blueprint because it was assumed that you would build your house someday,

3
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.440
and so you had to be able to work with the architect.

4
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:29.560
We had a course in home nursing because, as a mother, you would have to take care of your children.

5
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:38.860
We had a course in clothing construction and food preparation, the main things you did.

6
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:47.860
And so you could either teach these things as a home ec teacher or you could do them at home.

7
00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:56.820
That’s close to all the opportunities there were in home economics.

8
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:19.800
And then out of that grew a lot of experimental kitchens and a real time in the United States when it was really a big deal to . . . [flipping pages] Here’s some pictures of a Betty Crocker home economics kitchen with the home economist in it.

9
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.040
And see, you could be trained to do these consumer services, too.

10
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.760
So you were trained in how to give demonstrations.

11
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:35.000
We were the Julia Childs of the day and made all the demonstrations.
